.TH SYS_PROC_PERS_NAME 2 "2026-05-09" "Substrate" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
sys_proc_pers_name \- resolve a personality ID to a name
.SH LIBRARY
System library (libsys, \-lsys)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/sysinfo.h>
.PP
.BI "int sys_proc_pers_name(int " perso_id ", char *" buf ", size_t " len ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR sys_proc_pers_name ()
copies the human-readable name of personality
.I perso_id
into
.I buf .
The name is the same string the kernel uses internally
.RB ( perso_name ()
in
.BR sys/exec/perso/personality.c ),
e.g.
.BR \[dq]Native\[dq] ,
.BR \[dq]Linux\[dq] ,
.BR \[dq]FreeBSD\[dq] ,
.BR \[dq]NetBSD\[dq] .
Unknown IDs resolve to
.BR \[dq]unknown\[dq] .
.PP
The
.I len
parameter has classic probe semantics: pass
.I len
= 0 to learn the required buffer size (returned via the function
result) without writing anything.  When
.I len
is too small for the full string, the kernel writes
.IR "len - 1"
bytes followed by a NUL terminator.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, the byte length needed (including the NUL terminator).
On failure, a negated errno is returned.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I len
is non-zero but
.I buf
is NULL.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I buf
points outside the caller's address space.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_proc_info (2),
.BR uname (2),
.BR sysinfo (2)
